00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:42.760
in the amount of times these static
files were getting called on.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:46.840
And since this is a very diverse codebase,
mostly enterprise customers,

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:49.840
it's really hard
to kind of take out one specific takeaway.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:55.080
But I think this was
completely invisible to you.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:57.080
And I mean, we saw this in the data.

00:14:57.080 --> 00:14:58.600
And after what does this mean?

00:14:58.600 --> 00:15:03.000
Why has there been almost a 50% decrease in the amount of times

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:06.080
the agents use these
specific instruction files?

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:09.240
Again,
this is going to be a confusing term.

00:15:09.240 --> 00:15:12.240
This is not supposed to instill confidence in you

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:15.760
second or third graph right now.

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:20.640
So we have this way to see whether or not
our agent suggestions

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:25.160
are getting picked up
and addressed by humans or coding agents.

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:29.880
And this approach shows you two
different lines across this past summer,

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:34.080
which had multiple meaningful model releases,
by the way, no skewing models here.

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:38.440
And you can see, basically,
the amount of times

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:43.000
that we got addressed comments
with guidelines and without guidelines.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:48.120
So what we saw is that when agents
adhere to coding guidelines,

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:51.240
like the ones that exist
in those instruction files,

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:55.480
we see that eventually there's stronger
acceptance from developers.

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:57.800
So it's a strong correlation.
So what happens?

00:15:57.800 --> 00:15:59.840
People write their instruction files.

00:15:59.840 --> 00:16:01.320
Our agents pick them up.

00:16:01.320 --> 00:16:02.360
And then there's strong

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:06.200
adherence and acceptance
of of those findings specifically.

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:09.680
So it means that
when we do specifically try to find

00:16:09.960 --> 00:16:13.160
problems and bugs
using their instructions and skills.

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:15.920
The baseline model adheres to them.

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:18.840
And we're seeing better acceptance rates, with that in mind.

00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:21.720
We also saw a

00:16:21.720 --> 00:16:25.920
correlation where coding guidelines
correlate with higher review acceptance.

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:29.320
So we produce a review comment.
We tell you.

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:30.560
Hey there's going to be a bug here.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:32.440
There's going to be a vulnerability.

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:36.680
And we identify then whenever
we include your own guidelines, either

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:38.120
in the language or the sentiment,

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:41.120
there's a stronger likelihood
that developers will use it.

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:45.440
Now, this doesn't work with a stock harness — like your stock harness.

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:47.880
If you use Claude Code, you don't get this.

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:53.000
This happens with our harness because
we force the model to use your guidelines.

00:16:53.000 --> 00:16:56.000
Because we saw this data back
in the beginning of the summer,

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:59.600
that whenever we include language that exists in your instruction files,

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:04.040
there's a higher likelihood that it will get picked up.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:05.920
We think it's correlation.

00:17:05.920 --> 00:17:09.200
We have some examples that it's not causation.

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:11.960
Yeah.

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:13.520
So, what's the takeaway from this?

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:16.800
So one thing to think about
when you think about writing your own

00:17:16.840 --> 00:17:20.560
your own harness, your own AGENTS.md,

00:17:20.600 --> 00:17:24.440
your own skills
registry — is to really think deeply.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.680
If you are in a position
where you can continuously maintain

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:30.760
the knowledge and information
that's stored in that file.

00:17:30.920 --> 00:17:34.640
Because what happens is, regardless
whether or not it was updated recently,

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:38.560
other coding agents, downstream,
and Baz is no different

00:17:38.560 --> 00:17:41.920
than a GitHub Copilot, and it's no
different than a CodeRabbit or a Cursor.

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:45.440
They are going to pick up
on that little nuance and assume

00:17:45.440 --> 00:17:48.440
that's
still a single source of truth for you.

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:52.080
What's the inverse version of that?

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:54.480
If you have skills, you have guidelines.

00:17:54.480 --> 00:17:57.920
You have instruction files, especially
in a big team in a large mono repo.

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:02.120
And they weren't updated in the last six,
12, 18 weeks.

00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:05.320
And you didn't specifically
call them out on another,

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:11.480
kind of central control plane for your AI,

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:14.360
whether it's your own harness
or like a global ruleset,

00:18:14.360 --> 00:18:18.000
then you're going to get a self-correcting
agent

00:18:18.000 --> 00:18:22.480
that's continuously adhering to that old agent stack,

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:25.880
even if it's no longer relevant,
even if sometimes

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:29.080
you've made other decisions downstream
that don't necessarily reflect that.

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:35.960
And one final one just to close this off.

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:39.320
So again,
this data is supposed to be confusing.

00:18:39.880 --> 00:18:42.720
We discovered that some specific
coding instructions

00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:45.720
correlate stronger to high acceptance.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:49.200
So specifically, one of our competitors is called CodeRabbit.

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:54.160
They actually use a structured JSON
that contains a rule set.

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:55.960
Has anyone used CodeRabbit?

00:18:55.960 --> 00:18:56.280
Yeah.

00:18:56.280 --> 00:18:59.480
So CodeRabbit specifically,
we discovered that their rule

00:18:59.480 --> 00:19:03.000
set across models and across
organizations

00:19:03.120 --> 00:19:07.560
is specifically strong
at forcing your agents to do something.

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:12.880
So even if you did like a poke
or like a 14 day trial a year ago,

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:16.160
and this file still exists in your code
base, it's

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:21.080
going to overcome the instructions in your CLAUDE.md, your latest skills.

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:24.080
And even if you move to Cursor or an AGENTS.md.

00:19:24.320 --> 00:19:24.640
Right.

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:29.000
So think about the hydra problem here, and how much that makes a difference.

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:32.720
Okay.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:35.920
So to wrap this
up I want to send you off with two things.

00:19:37.240 --> 00:19:42.200
We started with steer steer and age. So.

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:47.520
If it was on a board

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:50.520
then someone would pick it up
and the task would get done.

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:55.080
We had rituals like a daily or weekly
or a retrospective

00:19:55.080 --> 00:19:58.160
where we talked about the good
and the bad practices that we want to

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:01.480
distill into our knowledge bases
and into our engineering habits.

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:03.640
Those are gone.

00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:06.440
I would say that across

00:20:06.440 --> 00:20:09.440
maybe thousands of teams that we've seen.

00:20:09.960 --> 00:20:12.680
Those rituals are diminishing, in the sense

00:20:12.680 --> 00:20:16.320
that — are they really determining what
the engineering outcomes are going to be?

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:18.680
So what replaced them?

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:20.040
Three main things.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.520
If I have to be very, very general.

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:24.080
The first one is spec.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:28.320
So huge growth in spec files.

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:32.960
Spec files written either in markdown or linked to a Linear issue.

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:37.040
And by the way, plan files as well.

00:20:37.040 --> 00:20:39.640
Like planning, and planning through Claude Code.

00:20:39.640 --> 00:20:42.680
Those are probably the number one ways to make

00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:45.760
your coding agents
act in a specific vertical.

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:48.520
Collaboration over planning.

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:50.440
Not completely solved today with Claude Code,

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:53.600
but there is a good open source solution you should check out — Plane.

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:57.480
As an example, we have some capability over there,

00:20:57.680 --> 00:21:01.280
but you'll be amazed at
how much a good human written

00:21:01.280 --> 00:21:05.520
spec impacts outcome

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:09.000
regardless of the underlying
configuration mess.

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:11.240
Number two is verification.

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:13.920
Obviously I'm biased
because that's my day job.

00:21:13.920 --> 00:21:17.920
I've been doing this for seven years,
but if we identify GitHub as like

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:20.920
this choke point, that's really important
for us for decision making.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:25.000
If we built the tools into either
a GitHub Action or,

00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:28.640
or a stock tool, where you get that
verification,

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:33.920
you'll be able to suppress bad behaviors that result from steer slips.

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:35.880
Number

00:21:35.880 --> 00:21:39.080
three is and this is quite
new is merge tactics.

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:42.280
So historically, we'd write, right?

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:46.400
We would squash and merge
the diffs into our main branch.

00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:52.880
I would strongly recommend
looking at creative merge tactics.

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:54.520
Merge queues are now native to GitHub.

00:21:54.520 --> 00:21:57.760
So that's not even
a niche product anymore.

00:21:57.800 --> 00:21:58.920
Anyone can use them.

00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:01.520
And what merge queues
specifically allow you to do.

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:04.040
And there's different ways to go about it.

00:22:04.040 --> 00:22:07.360
It allows you basically to cherry
pick much better than before

00:22:07.360 --> 00:22:11.160
and be able to understand whether or not,
especially if you wrote a good spec,

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:14.280
what you eventually
want your code to include.

00:22:14.360 --> 00:22:18.480
So, you know, one thing we're seeing
is that it probably doesn't make sense.

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:21.640
It's probably almost like a hopeless goal.

00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:25.880
You're not going to have the best hygiene
on your coding

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:29.840
agent operating system, just because this
environment is moving very fast.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:31.360
One thing that we recommend to

00:22:31.360 --> 00:22:34.360
our customers is to think, rethink
about collaboration and agile

00:22:34.680 --> 00:22:38.160
through these three specific lenses,
which we think are still top of mind

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:39.440
for companies,

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:41.480
especially if you're building
something like a software factory.

00:22:41.480 --> 00:22:44.880
But even if you're doing some baseline
modernization around the code agent.

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:47.600
And that's it.

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:49.920
If this is interesting for you.

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:52.920
There's one asset I would recommend checking out at baz.ai.

