00:24:35.560 --> 00:24:38.960
are you still seeing — even when the files setting

00:24:38.960 --> 00:24:42.440
get cut down for the guidance
and the improvement for the code reviews?

00:24:43.000 --> 00:24:46.200
Towards the end, I saw that there was
still a bit of an uptrend, right?

00:24:46.240 --> 00:24:50.200
Like we're still now compounding
and adding more files and stuff.

00:24:50.280 --> 00:24:54.160
Do you see that on average decreasing, or do we still see it going back to levels

00:24:54.160 --> 00:24:58.200
where the guidance that we're adding into
our code bases is just different?

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:02.000
Maybe less prescriptive
or even more specific.

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:05.400
Like, how's it changing as the models are getting better?

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:08.680
I'll tell you why —
that's the exact question

00:25:08.680 --> 00:25:11.680
that freaks me out and freaks
some of our customers out,

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:15.400
because there's anecdotal evidence that that's exactly what's happening.

00:25:15.400 --> 00:25:18.760
So later model versions are better

00:25:18.760 --> 00:25:22.320
at adhering to baseline instructions.

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:23.560
Let's call them.

00:25:23.560 --> 00:25:28.640
But the problem, I think, is that we
never pin to a specific model.

00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:32.000
So think about even today, especially

00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:35.720
at the meta level, like the Opus 5 models came out,

00:25:36.120 --> 00:25:40.720
we very rarely used the same model
throughout our coding journey.

00:25:40.840 --> 00:25:43.560
We would usually start
a, you know, plan with maybe

00:25:47.080 --> 00:25:47.760
get bigger model.

00:25:47.760 --> 00:25:49.480
Then, you know, another model

00:25:49.480 --> 00:25:52.160
maybe doesn't have that problem
because you have great investors.

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:55.560
But many, many of us
and many of our customers have that.

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:58.640
The reason is that

00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:02.200
the inconsistency has too many variables.

00:26:02.480 --> 00:26:05.600
So the models are constantly
changing under the hood,

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:09.120
the operating systems and the paradigms
are changing under the hood.

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:14.040
And my concern is that the labs are not very transparent

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:19.840
about how they ingest and inject, that
type of context in and out of the session.

00:26:19.880 --> 00:26:21.400
So even if I want to make a bold

00:26:21.400 --> 00:26:25.240
investment and say, you know,
our team is going to be completely

00:26:25.440 --> 00:26:28.920
invested in skills
and build their own skills registry.

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:32.320
But OpenAI then comes out with a model
that does a better job

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:34.000
at compaction and injecting,

00:26:34.000 --> 00:26:35.960
you know, 
skills into the context session,

00:26:35.960 --> 00:26:39.040
but we don't know how they do it
on the file level, on the folder level, on

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:42.400
the repo level,
that I think it's still a big mystery.

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:44.280
And people
are going to have to figure it out

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:46.200
whether it works out
for their code base or not.

00:26:46.200 --> 00:26:50.560
And the problem is that we don't have good
visibility to adherence at the moment.

