how should we think about interpreting,

00:27:30.440 --> 00:27:33.800
kind of the data that we've seen so far

00:27:33.880 --> 00:27:37.440
and maybe what to expect that might change

00:27:37.440 --> 00:27:40.560
or just, you know, how do we think
about that additional complexity?

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:41.600
Yeah. Great. Great point.

00:27:41.600 --> 00:27:44.400
So the question was on a long-horizon task.

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:46.520
We have one good example
that's pretty consistent.

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:53.080
We've been doing full code base sweeps
for a good part of the last ten months.

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:56.440
So like a chunky database
that gives us an indication on whether

00:27:56.440 --> 00:28:01.040
or not models and instruction files are really adhering, in the long horizon.

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:05.640
Much more consistent than shorter tasks.

00:28:05.960 --> 00:28:10.120
And we have a reason
to think why that's happening

00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:11.560
just because of the amount of turns

00:28:11.560 --> 00:28:15.680
and iterations, there's just a larger
likelihood that your coding agents

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:21.080
are going to land on the right instruction
file within that longer time period,

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:25.440
but we're seeing a sharp decrease
on the user side.

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:28.440
So users
don't want to spend a lot of time coding.

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:30.640
They're expecting faster responses.

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:34.240
They don't want to use those expensive and long-horizon models right now.

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:38.040
So I feel like the trade-off right now
is between

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:41.320
you know, longer horizon, like, go on this board.

00:28:41.320 --> 00:28:45.240
Side quests probably get better adherence,
but on the other hand,

00:28:45.240 --> 00:28:48.680
I think the developers downstream don't really want that.

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:49.240
They want fast

00:28:49.240 --> 00:28:53.120
responses, they want faster verification,
they want to close faster loops.

00:28:53.480 --> 00:28:56.960
And that's where these problems
are really at their worst.

